A||||3||WisconsinGT||Rich mixture help||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||05-20-2000||02:49 AM||||What are the sensors that send info. to the ECM regarding fuel mixture? I am going to try and figure out why my mixture is rich over the weekend. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||ounmongk||05-20-2000||02:59 AM||||good old Hayes: <BR>" Ecessive fuel consuption: <BR> 1 Dirty or clogged air filter element<BR> 2 Incorrectly set ignition timing <BR> 3 Emissions systems not functioning properly<BR> 4 Fuel injection internal parts excessivly worn or damaged<BR> 5 low tire pressure or incorrect tire size" <P>sensor-wise, off the top of my head, I can think of O2 sensor and the 'stat... <BR>Also, how can I tell if the engine's running rich? is it by milage? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||05-20-2000||03:15 AM||||The O2 sensor controls rich/lean. But the other things I know that can cause a rich condition are clogged air filter or a catalytic converter that is clogging up.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||05-20-2000||11:05 PM||||The O2 is the biggest controller of mixture, then TPS, then MAP, then coolant sensor, then intake air temperature sensor.<P>Those control the fuel mixture.<P>If it's rich, the first thing I would suspect is the O2 since it can go lazy and not set a code. They get real slow. The ECM still gets some cross counts and doesn't set a code.<P>Do you have any error codes?<BR>||||reg||1
